WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.004
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.004 --> 00:00:05.670
YURI GRINSHTEYN:
Welcome to Cloud on Air

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:09.170
live webinars from Google
Cloud, and this is CE TV.

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:10.835
We're hosting webinars
every Tuesday.

00:00:10.835 --> 00:00:11.960
My name is Yuri Grinshteyn.

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:14.670
I'm a Cloud customer
engineer here at Google,

00:00:14.670 --> 00:00:17.980
and today joined by Spoons, the
CTO and co-founder of LightStep

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:21.510
to talk about observability
and distributed applications.

00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:23.470
You can ask questions
anytime on the platform,

00:00:23.470 --> 00:00:26.560
and we have folks on standby
ready to answer them.

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:27.924
So let's get started.

00:00:27.924 --> 00:00:29.340
So first of all,
Spoons, thank you

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:30.240
so much for being here today.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:30.790
We appreciate it.

00:00:30.790 --> 00:00:31.080
DANIEL SPOONHOWER:
Yeah, my pleasure.

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:31.840
It's great to be here.

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:32.229
YURI GRINSHTEYN: All right.

00:00:32.229 --> 00:00:34.080
So let's talk about
microservices.

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:34.410
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: Sure.

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:36.035
YURI GRINSHTEYN: I
think a lot of folks

00:00:36.035 --> 00:00:38.730
think of them as kind of
the ultimate answer to all

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:39.770
of their problems.

00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:40.520
What do you think?

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:41.190
DANIEL SPOONHOWER:
Yeah, there's a lot

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:43.620
of great things about
microservices, serverless,

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:45.600
other sort of modern
architectures.

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:46.854
I think it lets you scale.

00:00:46.854 --> 00:00:49.020
It lets you divide the work
among your team in a lot

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:51.476
of ways, so yeah it's great.

00:00:51.476 --> 00:00:53.100
YURI GRINSHTEYN:
Yeah, but I'm assuming

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:55.050
that we wouldn't be here
today if it were perfect.

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:56.383
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: That's right.

00:00:56.383 --> 00:00:58.050
I think as much as
that microservices

00:00:58.050 --> 00:01:00.630
help you move quickly,
you kind of often

00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:02.880
lose the big picture
for what's actually

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:04.400
happening in your application.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:05.010
YURI GRINSHTEYN:
Yeah, and I think

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:07.301
one of the ways you lose it--
sort of one of the issues

00:01:07.301 --> 00:01:09.450
you have is around
observability, right?

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:11.367
And the more services
you have, the more stuff

00:01:11.367 --> 00:01:13.408
you have talking to each
other-- the harder it is

00:01:13.408 --> 00:01:14.800
to understand what's going on.

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:15.900
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: Yeah,
especially if you kind of step

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:17.520
back and think of it from
your users' point of view.

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:19.830
Users don't care how many
microservices are involved.

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:21.770
They just care about the
experience they have,

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:24.270
and that's going to cut across
all of your services in a way

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:26.297
that maybe your
traditional tools are not

00:01:26.297 --> 00:01:27.130
taking into account.

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:29.700
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Yeah, so what
are some of the observability

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:32.811
challenges that you've seen with
microservices architectures?

00:01:32.811 --> 00:01:34.560
DANIEL SPOONHOWER:
Yeah, I think it really

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:36.143
starts from just the
tools themselves,

00:01:36.143 --> 00:01:42.690
so I think when you think about
what's happening-- you know,

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:44.790
we saw this tweet that
I think is pretty funny.

00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:47.180
"I replaced our monolith
with microservices,

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:49.580
and that turns every outage
into a murder mystery."

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:51.330
That's really kind of
coming from the fact

00:01:51.330 --> 00:01:54.270
that people kind of look at
these three tools in terms--

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:58.470
or three sets of tools-- of
how they look at observability.

00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:00.360
Metrics, logging,
and tracing are

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:03.450
sort of three approaches to how
you might see what's going on

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:04.780
inside the application itself.

00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:05.960
YURI GRINSHTEYN: So let's
talk about those three.

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:07.230
So first, metrics, right?

00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:10.599
They're time series data,
what are they useful for?

00:02:10.599 --> 00:02:12.390
What do they tell us
about what's happening

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:13.349
within our application?

00:02:13.349 --> 00:02:14.848
DANIEL SPOONHOWER:
Yeah, metrics are

00:02:14.848 --> 00:02:17.400
great for understanding when
something has gone wrong.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:20.070
It's easy to set thresholds,
alerts, things like that.

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:21.584
You can get notified.

00:02:21.584 --> 00:02:23.250
They're not so great
about understanding

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:24.374
why something's gone wrong.

00:02:24.374 --> 00:02:26.820
So you know it's easy to tell
that it might be correlated

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:28.903
failures, but understanding
the causes of failures

00:02:28.903 --> 00:02:29.800
can be pretty hard.

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:30.150
YURI GRINSHTEYN: All right.

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:32.483
So yeah, I think it's a good
way of describing it right.

00:02:32.483 --> 00:02:34.140
Metrics tell us when
something happens.

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:34.720
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: Yeah.

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:37.010
YURI GRINSHTEYN: So how do we
find out what is happening?

00:02:37.010 --> 00:02:39.000
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: So again,
traditionally, maybe you

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:41.116
look at a logging tool to
do something like that.

00:02:41.116 --> 00:02:42.990
Logging is great about
giving a ton of detail

00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:45.120
about a specific
transaction or maybe

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:49.479
within a specific
service, and yeah it

00:02:49.479 --> 00:02:50.520
can give you that detail.

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:54.867
I think the downside of it
is really the volume of data

00:02:54.867 --> 00:02:55.950
that you might have there.

00:02:55.950 --> 00:02:56.866
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Sure.

00:02:56.866 --> 00:02:59.130
All right, so we got
metrics, and we've got logs.

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:00.210
What about traces?

00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:00.750
DANIEL SPOONHOWER:
Traces are good.

00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:02.333
It's sort of the new
kid on the block,

00:03:02.333 --> 00:03:04.840
I feel like, in
this in some ways.

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:07.230
One of my co-founders
built a system at Google

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:09.540
called Dapper, which was
Google's distributed tracing

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:10.039
system.

00:03:10.039 --> 00:03:11.940
And Dapper was great
for understanding

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:14.250
the latency and some
of the other issues

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:18.270
that were coming up
in Google's web search

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:20.520
and able to really,
like, identify,

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:23.310
for a particular search,
where was the latency?

00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:24.920
Where was the
slowness coming from?

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:27.128
You do a search as a user--
you don't think about it,

00:03:27.128 --> 00:03:30.870
but there is web docs, there's
news, there's personal results,

00:03:30.870 --> 00:03:31.560
there's images.

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:33.320
There's all kinds of things that
are sort of feeding into that.

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:35.220
Any one of those things could
be a problem at any time.

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:35.790
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Sure.

00:03:35.790 --> 00:03:37.400
All right, so we've
got our metrics

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:39.720
to tell us what is going on.

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:41.550
We've got logs that
allow us to understand

00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:43.700
errors and specific events.

00:03:43.700 --> 00:03:46.620
We got traces that tell
us what our latency is,

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:48.250
and then we have
distributed tracing,

00:03:48.250 --> 00:03:49.410
which kind of tells
us what's the latency

00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:50.520
between different microservices.

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:51.030
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: Yep.

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:51.870
YURI GRINSHTEYN:
All right, so why

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:53.400
is observability
still a problem?

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:54.510
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: Yes,
so each of these things

00:03:54.510 --> 00:03:56.680
has kind of their own
problems in a way,

00:03:56.680 --> 00:03:59.790
so really to take
advantage of metrics

00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:02.440
you want to be able to dig
in a bit and understand

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:05.310
is this problem only occurring
in the latest releases

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:07.030
or incurred in all releases?

00:04:07.030 --> 00:04:08.544
OK, well, how do
I do that, right?

00:04:08.544 --> 00:04:09.210
I tag it, right?

00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:10.680
I put a tag on
the data, so now I

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:13.050
can sort of segment
by different releases.

00:04:13.050 --> 00:04:15.974
I probably also want to segment
maybe by host or data center,

00:04:15.974 --> 00:04:18.390
so I can figure out if there's
some sort of infrastructure

00:04:18.390 --> 00:04:20.220
problem.

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:22.770
A lot of folks,
us included, we'll

00:04:22.770 --> 00:04:25.530
tag based upon customer IDs, but
these things start adding up--

00:04:25.530 --> 00:04:27.530
YURI GRINSHTEYN: [INAUDIBLE]
a lot of customers.

00:04:27.530 --> 00:04:28.290
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: Yes, exactly.

00:04:28.290 --> 00:04:31.004
And when you think about the way
that a metrics tool is built,

00:04:31.004 --> 00:04:32.670
it really has to keep
track of every one

00:04:32.670 --> 00:04:33.990
of these individual streams--

00:04:33.990 --> 00:04:35.790
and really, the
kind of full cross

00:04:35.790 --> 00:04:38.320
product of all those things--
every different combination.

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:39.819
And this is what
folks kind of refer

00:04:39.819 --> 00:04:42.030
to as sort of the cardinality
problem, in a sense.

00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:42.650
YURI GRINSHTEYN:
Sure, all right.

00:04:42.650 --> 00:04:44.970
So metrics are good,
because they tell us

00:04:44.970 --> 00:04:46.121
when things are happening.

00:04:46.121 --> 00:04:47.620
But it's hard to
keep track of them,

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:49.244
especially if you've
got a big system--

00:04:49.244 --> 00:04:51.820
lots of services, lots of
customers, lots of metrics.

00:04:51.820 --> 00:04:52.320
All right.

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:54.450
Well, surely logs don't
have any problems.

00:04:54.450 --> 00:04:57.250
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: Yeah,
they might have a few.

00:04:57.250 --> 00:04:59.100
So logs are great,
but you really

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:01.600
end up paying by volume
a lot of the time.

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:03.730
And if the amount of logging
that you're generating

00:05:03.730 --> 00:05:06.010
is just proportional to
the amount of service

00:05:06.010 --> 00:05:08.494
that you're providing-- cool,
that's just like fixed costs.

00:05:08.494 --> 00:05:09.160
It's no problem.

00:05:09.160 --> 00:05:10.720
You can think about that.

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:12.670
But as the complexity
or service grows,

00:05:12.670 --> 00:05:14.890
and you start building
out similar microservices

00:05:14.890 --> 00:05:16.600
or similar architecture,
you're going

00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:19.150
to want to add more logging
for each new microservice

00:05:19.150 --> 00:05:20.180
that you add.

00:05:20.180 --> 00:05:21.700
So now the amount of logging
that you're generating

00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:23.440
is not just proportional
to the traffic

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:24.980
that you're serving
to your users

00:05:24.980 --> 00:05:27.287
but also to the complexity
of what you're building.

00:05:27.287 --> 00:05:29.620
And that means either your
costs are going to go way up,

00:05:29.620 --> 00:05:31.420
or if you fix your
costs you're going

00:05:31.420 --> 00:05:33.790
to have to reduce the amount
of logging that you do.

00:05:33.790 --> 00:05:35.530
YURI GRINSHTEYN: So you have to
look at things like sampling.

00:05:35.530 --> 00:05:37.113
DANIEL SPOONHOWER:
Yes, or even just--

00:05:37.113 --> 00:05:39.329
I know folks that won't
log in some environments.

00:05:39.329 --> 00:05:41.620
Like, they just don't log in
their staging environment,

00:05:41.620 --> 00:05:43.360
because it's too expensive.

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:44.260
That's unfortunate,
because, you know,

00:05:44.260 --> 00:05:46.120
that could be really valuable
in diagnosing problems

00:05:46.120 --> 00:05:47.060
in that environment.

00:05:47.060 --> 00:05:48.550
YURI GRINSHTEYN: It's hard
to reproduce a problem

00:05:48.550 --> 00:05:50.740
outside of production if you
don't have logging turned on

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:51.630
in those environments.

00:05:51.630 --> 00:05:53.005
DANIEL SPOONHOWER:
Yeah, exactly.

00:05:53.005 --> 00:05:54.160
Exactly.

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:56.800
YURI GRINSHTEYN: All right,
so logs don't necessarily

00:05:56.800 --> 00:05:58.132
scale as well.

00:05:58.132 --> 00:05:59.590
All right, let's
talk about traces.

00:05:59.590 --> 00:06:00.370
What are the issues there?

00:06:00.370 --> 00:06:01.370
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: Yeah.

00:06:01.370 --> 00:06:04.872
Yeah, so at least in the way
traces were built in Google

00:06:04.872 --> 00:06:06.580
and the way that they
are built in Dapper

00:06:06.580 --> 00:06:09.730
is they were actually looking at
a pretty narrow problem, which

00:06:09.730 --> 00:06:11.290
is looking at--
for a web search,

00:06:11.290 --> 00:06:13.944
how do we understand
overall latency?

00:06:13.944 --> 00:06:15.610
And it turns out that
Google does, like,

00:06:15.610 --> 00:06:17.224
a bazillion requests
every second,

00:06:17.224 --> 00:06:19.390
and so you can actually
throw away a lot of the data

00:06:19.390 --> 00:06:21.347
and still get a
pretty good signal.

00:06:21.347 --> 00:06:22.930
And so, for Dapper
actually, the costs

00:06:22.930 --> 00:06:25.360
were not in terms of the
overhead in the application

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:29.530
itself, so that is, like,
in web search Google still,

00:06:29.530 --> 00:06:32.530
I think anyway, basically
doing instrumentation

00:06:32.530 --> 00:06:33.940
every request that comes in.

00:06:33.940 --> 00:06:34.940
The thing that turns
out to be expensive

00:06:34.940 --> 00:06:36.110
is actually just storing
all of that data.

00:06:36.110 --> 00:06:36.740
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Oh
yeah, that makes sense.

00:06:36.740 --> 00:06:38.260
I could see how that
could be a lot of data.

00:06:38.260 --> 00:06:40.060
DANIEL SPOONHOWER:
Yeah, so within Dapper,

00:06:40.060 --> 00:06:43.690
kind of the default mode
anyway is that you throw out

00:06:43.690 --> 00:06:47.320
99.9% of the data
almost immediately,

00:06:47.320 --> 00:06:49.300
and then you throw
out another 90%.

00:06:49.300 --> 00:06:51.975
So you're down to
99.99% of the data as

00:06:51.975 --> 00:06:54.350
thrown out, so you just have
this tiny fraction of what's

00:06:54.350 --> 00:06:54.850
left.

00:06:54.850 --> 00:06:57.340
And again, like, 0.1% of a
bazillion is still like--

00:06:57.340 --> 00:06:58.060
YURI GRINSHTEYN: It's a lot.

00:06:58.060 --> 00:06:58.910
DANIEL SPOONHOWER:
--a big number.

00:06:58.910 --> 00:06:59.890
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Yeah.

00:06:59.890 --> 00:07:00.790
DANIEL SPOONHOWER:
But now you start

00:07:00.790 --> 00:07:02.740
to miss out on some of the
finer detail, where it's like,

00:07:02.740 --> 00:07:05.080
OK, well, if you're just
looking in aggregate across all

00:07:05.080 --> 00:07:06.371
of Google's [INAUDIBLE], great.

00:07:06.371 --> 00:07:08.950
But if we want to start talking
about maybe a different use

00:07:08.950 --> 00:07:11.742
case, where you've got
these interesting segments

00:07:11.742 --> 00:07:12.700
of different customers.

00:07:12.700 --> 00:07:14.870
You're interested in
sort of a small fraction

00:07:14.870 --> 00:07:15.824
of your customers.

00:07:15.824 --> 00:07:17.740
The chances of that small
fraction overlapping

00:07:17.740 --> 00:07:19.670
with this 0.1% are
starting to get small.

00:07:19.670 --> 00:07:20.990
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Yeah, so I
can see some of the challenges

00:07:20.990 --> 00:07:22.531
that we're trying
to identify, right.

00:07:22.531 --> 00:07:25.300
It's hard to keep track of
lots of different metrics.

00:07:25.300 --> 00:07:28.150
It's hard to keep all
of the logs just because

00:07:28.150 --> 00:07:30.750
of the amount of data we have
to store, and how much it costs.

00:07:30.750 --> 00:07:33.700
You know, whether you're
paying your infrastructure

00:07:33.700 --> 00:07:35.560
costs to yourself
in your data center

00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:39.070
or to someone like Google to
store them, it's still a lot.

00:07:39.070 --> 00:07:42.180
And then traces also create
a lot of data, is that right?

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:43.180
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: Yeah.

00:07:43.180 --> 00:07:44.620
So I think you actually
hit it right there.

00:07:44.620 --> 00:07:47.203
I mean, the way that we kind of
think about these at LightStep

00:07:47.203 --> 00:07:49.514
is that metrics, logs,
and traces are just data.

00:07:49.514 --> 00:07:51.430
And as much as that
people kind of in the past

00:07:51.430 --> 00:07:53.305
have thought about it
as three separate tools

00:07:53.305 --> 00:07:55.030
that you've got
in your tool belt,

00:07:55.030 --> 00:07:58.780
there's a lot of opportunities
to really look at

00:07:58.780 --> 00:08:01.510
where you can use them
to interact and leverage

00:08:01.510 --> 00:08:04.300
aspects of metrics, and
how you do log collection--

00:08:04.300 --> 00:08:06.040
look at how log
collection-- or how logs

00:08:06.040 --> 00:08:07.530
can be combined with traces.

00:08:07.530 --> 00:08:10.030
And that way, you can sort of
look more holistically at what

00:08:10.030 --> 00:08:11.530
are the problems
that we're actually

00:08:11.530 --> 00:08:13.239
trying to solve as an
observability tool?

00:08:13.239 --> 00:08:14.696
YURI GRINSHTEYN:
So now what I feel

00:08:14.696 --> 00:08:16.770
like I'm the person in
the infomercial, who's

00:08:16.770 --> 00:08:17.440
like struggling.

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:19.990
I'm, like, dropping things, or
I'm trying to chop vegetables.

00:08:19.990 --> 00:08:21.290
I don't know what I'm doing.

00:08:21.290 --> 00:08:22.440
And then the announcer
comes on and says,

00:08:22.440 --> 00:08:24.070
you know, surely, there
must be a better way?

00:08:24.070 --> 00:08:24.790
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: There
is a knife for that.

00:08:24.790 --> 00:08:26.690
YURI GRINSHTEYN: All right, so
tell me about the better way.

00:08:26.690 --> 00:08:27.160
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: Sure.

00:08:27.160 --> 00:08:29.210
So let's just do
kind of a quick demo.

00:08:29.210 --> 00:08:33.770
So this is sort of one view into
what we can show in LightStep,

00:08:33.770 --> 00:08:36.799
so this is kind of a demo
app that we've put together.

00:08:36.799 --> 00:08:39.710
So what we're doing is we're
monitoring application here,

00:08:39.710 --> 00:08:42.710
which does some sort
of reservation system.

00:08:42.710 --> 00:08:45.450
I don't know if it's cars, or
seats, or tickets, or something

00:08:45.450 --> 00:08:45.950
like that.

00:08:45.950 --> 00:08:47.920
But anyway, there's an app.

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:50.596
There's a bunch of
microservices on the back end.

00:08:50.596 --> 00:08:51.970
And what we can
do is we can look

00:08:51.970 --> 00:08:53.600
at it across a bunch of
different dimensions,

00:08:53.600 --> 00:08:55.570
so here we're looking at a
particular set of requests that

00:08:55.570 --> 00:08:56.771
are coming from the iOS app.

00:08:56.771 --> 00:08:58.770
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Things
look pretty flat, right?

00:08:58.770 --> 00:09:01.184
Everything seems to be
consistent and fine here?

00:09:01.184 --> 00:09:03.100
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: Yes,
so sort of three parts

00:09:03.100 --> 00:09:06.460
of what we're doing on the
top-- looking at latency, which

00:09:06.460 --> 00:09:09.070
is sort of the
slowness-- how long it's

00:09:09.070 --> 00:09:10.360
taking to respond to requests.

00:09:10.360 --> 00:09:12.130
Below that, the
rate of requests--

00:09:12.130 --> 00:09:13.660
and then below that,
the error rate.

00:09:13.660 --> 00:09:14.680
So rate's pretty steady.

00:09:14.680 --> 00:09:16.850
No errors are happening,
so that's all good.

00:09:16.850 --> 00:09:19.567
And looking at latency,
yeah, it's pretty flat.

00:09:19.567 --> 00:09:21.400
What we're doing here
is we're only showing,

00:09:21.400 --> 00:09:22.930
though, the median latency.

00:09:22.930 --> 00:09:24.160
YURI GRINSHTEYN: OK.

00:09:24.160 --> 00:09:25.743
DANIEL SPOONHOWER:
So that's basically

00:09:25.743 --> 00:09:28.830
sort for 50 percentile
on what we're doing.

00:09:28.830 --> 00:09:31.207
If we sort of step
back a little bit,

00:09:31.207 --> 00:09:33.040
we can actually, in
LightStep, look not just

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:35.260
at the 50th percentile
but look at these higher

00:09:35.260 --> 00:09:36.250
percentiles above that.

00:09:36.250 --> 00:09:38.920
So we can look at 95th
percentile, 99th percentile,

00:09:38.920 --> 00:09:40.896
or even 99.9th percentile.

00:09:40.896 --> 00:09:42.520
When we look at those
actually, there's

00:09:42.520 --> 00:09:44.560
a lot more story to tell
about what's going on here.

00:09:44.560 --> 00:09:45.280
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Yes,
so this is actually

00:09:45.280 --> 00:09:46.660
showing you a very
different picture, right?

00:09:46.660 --> 00:09:48.730
Even though our median
latency is very flat,

00:09:48.730 --> 00:09:50.647
and there's really
nothing happening there.

00:09:50.647 --> 00:09:52.480
When we start going to
the high percentiles,

00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:55.120
it really looks like
there was an event here.

00:09:55.120 --> 00:09:58.990
There was a latency spike, then
latency sort of almost crashed,

00:09:58.990 --> 00:10:02.020
and then it evened down back
to kind of its expected value.

00:10:02.020 --> 00:10:03.020
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: Yeah.

00:10:03.020 --> 00:10:04.180
YURI GRINSHTEYN: So that's
a very different picture

00:10:04.180 --> 00:10:05.830
that you get when you look
at those higher percentiles

00:10:05.830 --> 00:10:07.630
than what you get when you're
just looking at the median.

00:10:07.630 --> 00:10:08.440
DANIEL SPOONHOWER:
Yeah, and that's I

00:10:08.440 --> 00:10:10.630
think super important,
especially in a distributed

00:10:10.630 --> 00:10:11.890
system.

00:10:11.890 --> 00:10:14.430
One of the reasons is that
these high percentile latencies,

00:10:14.430 --> 00:10:15.847
they are often
sort of bellwethers

00:10:15.847 --> 00:10:18.305
for what's going to happen for
most of your users later on.

00:10:18.305 --> 00:10:19.870
So these are really
users that are

00:10:19.870 --> 00:10:22.060
kind of pushing the
envelope, in a way,

00:10:22.060 --> 00:10:24.310
and as your overall
traffic grows,

00:10:24.310 --> 00:10:26.735
hopefully, it's not just
going to be 1% of users

00:10:26.735 --> 00:10:27.860
that are experiencing that.

00:10:27.860 --> 00:10:29.320
It's going to be more, but
if he can get ahead of it

00:10:29.320 --> 00:10:31.840
and fix it for those 1% before
it becomes a wider spread

00:10:31.840 --> 00:10:32.797
problem, that's great.

00:10:32.797 --> 00:10:35.380
The second thing is when you're
building a distributed system,

00:10:35.380 --> 00:10:37.300
you know, we're really just
looking at one component here.

00:10:37.300 --> 00:10:39.850
And we're looking and seeing
that 1% of the requests

00:10:39.850 --> 00:10:41.945
to that particular
component are slow,

00:10:41.945 --> 00:10:44.070
but if you think about it
from a user's perspective

00:10:44.070 --> 00:10:45.430
their transaction,
their request,

00:10:45.430 --> 00:10:47.596
is going to hit a whole
bunch of different services.

00:10:47.596 --> 00:10:49.912
So if each of those
has 1% that's low,

00:10:49.912 --> 00:10:51.370
the chances that
a user is actually

00:10:51.370 --> 00:10:53.828
having a bad experience are
going to go up, and up, and up.

00:10:53.828 --> 00:10:56.387
So maybe in an individual
service it's only 1%

00:10:56.387 --> 00:10:57.970
of requests, but,
you know, if there's

00:10:57.970 --> 00:11:00.680
20 services you know it's going
to be 20% of requests that are

00:11:00.680 --> 00:11:01.990
going to be slow for the user.

00:11:01.990 --> 00:11:03.740
YURI GRINSHTEYN: All right,
so next obvious question--

00:11:03.740 --> 00:11:05.751
I know that we're having
some latency issues.

00:11:05.751 --> 00:11:06.250
Why?

00:11:06.250 --> 00:11:06.950
What's going on?

00:11:06.950 --> 00:11:09.550
DANIEL SPOONHOWER:
Yeah, so I think--

00:11:09.550 --> 00:11:12.320
I kind of think of the
latency measurements

00:11:12.320 --> 00:11:14.680
that LightStep does is kind
of table stakes in a way.

00:11:14.680 --> 00:11:15.520
Like, you just got to do this.

00:11:15.520 --> 00:11:17.890
I think one of the cool things
that LightStep adds on top

00:11:17.890 --> 00:11:19.348
is that we're
actually also keeping

00:11:19.348 --> 00:11:22.140
a record of transactions
across these differently,

00:11:22.140 --> 00:11:25.030
and it's actually using the
latency as a kind of signal

00:11:25.030 --> 00:11:27.370
to tell what might be an
interesting transaction

00:11:27.370 --> 00:11:29.200
to go look at.

00:11:29.200 --> 00:11:31.739
So each of these
little dots here

00:11:31.739 --> 00:11:33.780
represents some transaction,
where we've captured

00:11:33.780 --> 00:11:35.140
a complete trace for that.

00:11:35.140 --> 00:11:37.250
YURI GRINSHTEYN: OK, so
that's a lot of traces.

00:11:37.250 --> 00:11:38.080
DANIEL SPOONHOWER:
Yeah, I mean, we

00:11:38.080 --> 00:11:39.820
pick what we think is a kind
of representative sample.

00:11:39.820 --> 00:11:42.190
We don't expect a human
to look at all of these.

00:11:42.190 --> 00:11:43.606
The way that I
kind of think of it

00:11:43.606 --> 00:11:47.332
is like, sort of, automated
crime scene investigations.

00:11:47.332 --> 00:11:48.790
So, you know, you're
the detective.

00:11:48.790 --> 00:11:50.331
You know that
something's gone wrong.

00:11:50.331 --> 00:11:54.277
You've got this robot that'll
come in and do this like--

00:11:54.277 --> 00:11:56.110
you know, both-- you
know, get good coverage

00:11:56.110 --> 00:11:58.390
kind of evidence
gathering but also

00:11:58.390 --> 00:11:59.650
be a little bit intelligent.

00:11:59.650 --> 00:12:02.695
It's not going to help you to
get, like, 10,000 fingerprints

00:12:02.695 --> 00:12:03.695
if all you is 10, right?

00:12:03.695 --> 00:12:05.861
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Right, you
want the important ones.

00:12:05.861 --> 00:12:06.990
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: Exactly.

00:12:06.990 --> 00:12:08.080
In this, we actually
try to bias.

00:12:08.080 --> 00:12:10.210
Like, if we were just
picking sort of arbitrarily,

00:12:10.210 --> 00:12:12.126
most of them would be
down here at the bottom.

00:12:12.126 --> 00:12:14.740
In fact, half of them would
be below the bottom line.

00:12:14.740 --> 00:12:16.940
We actually biased
towards these longer ones,

00:12:16.940 --> 00:12:19.570
so even though these requests
way up kind at the top here

00:12:19.570 --> 00:12:23.740
represent, say, only 0.01%,
0.05% of the requests,

00:12:23.740 --> 00:12:26.210
we're going to capture those
more frequently than that.

00:12:26.210 --> 00:12:27.760
YURI GRINSHTEYN: OK, so tell
me more about that biasing,

00:12:27.760 --> 00:12:29.801
because that sounds like
something very different

00:12:29.801 --> 00:12:31.796
than I've seen other
monitoring tools do.

00:12:31.796 --> 00:12:34.420
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: Yeah, it's a
little bit tricky to get right,

00:12:34.420 --> 00:12:36.336
and it's actually kind
of feeds into the built

00:12:36.336 --> 00:12:37.510
about the way we built it.

00:12:37.510 --> 00:12:40.270
But really, the idea
is that you want

00:12:40.270 --> 00:12:42.700
to understand as much about
the transaction as possible

00:12:42.700 --> 00:12:44.050
before you decide
whether or not it's

00:12:44.050 --> 00:12:45.475
going to be something
that a human's going

00:12:45.475 --> 00:12:46.030
to want to look at.

00:12:46.030 --> 00:12:46.900
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Sure,
that makes sense.

00:12:46.900 --> 00:12:49.610
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: And slowness
is a great signal to use.

00:12:49.610 --> 00:12:50.950
Unfortunately, you don't
know if a request is slow

00:12:50.950 --> 00:12:51.640
until it's done.

00:12:51.640 --> 00:12:52.480
YURI GRINSHTEYN: That's right.

00:12:52.480 --> 00:12:54.470
And you need to be
capturing the trace as it's

00:12:54.470 --> 00:12:55.720
happening, that way it's done.

00:12:55.720 --> 00:12:56.800
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: Exactly,
so there's a kind of time

00:12:56.800 --> 00:12:58.990
travel that we have to
do to make that happen,

00:12:58.990 --> 00:13:00.610
and this is pretty different
than the way you have your--

00:13:00.610 --> 00:13:01.510
YURI GRINSHTEYN: So you've
finally-- you did it?

00:13:01.510 --> 00:13:03.850
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: Yeah, in
a pretty narrow case but we

00:13:03.850 --> 00:13:05.262
got a kind of time travel.

00:13:05.262 --> 00:13:06.970
The way Dapper kind
of took this approach

00:13:06.970 --> 00:13:08.620
is that-- like again,
there's a bazillion requests.

00:13:08.620 --> 00:13:09.500
They're all kind of the same.

00:13:09.500 --> 00:13:10.500
It doesn't matter which.

00:13:10.500 --> 00:13:13.270
They actually made this decision
right when the request started,

00:13:13.270 --> 00:13:14.590
and we make it at the end.

00:13:14.590 --> 00:13:17.547
Or even, it can be seconds or
minutes after the request ends,

00:13:17.547 --> 00:13:19.630
so where there's something
else that's interesting

00:13:19.630 --> 00:13:21.769
that we think is sort of
relevant to a human trying

00:13:21.769 --> 00:13:22.810
to debug what's going on.

00:13:22.810 --> 00:13:24.018
YURI GRINSHTEYN: OK, so cool.

00:13:24.018 --> 00:13:26.412
So now show me what-- what
are we seeing when we're

00:13:26.412 --> 00:13:27.620
capturing these transactions?

00:13:27.620 --> 00:13:28.390
DANIEL SPOONHOWER:
Yes, so let me dig in

00:13:28.390 --> 00:13:29.348
on one of these things.

00:13:29.348 --> 00:13:31.930
This is a request that
took about seven seconds,

00:13:31.930 --> 00:13:33.690
and we're showing
kind of a trace

00:13:33.690 --> 00:13:36.020
of-- we can think of it
like kind of a Gantt chart,

00:13:36.020 --> 00:13:39.520
or we've got Chrome Debugger
or something like that.

00:13:39.520 --> 00:13:43.150
At the top, what we have is
sort of a summary of what's

00:13:43.150 --> 00:13:45.250
going on, breaking down
each of the services

00:13:45.250 --> 00:13:46.130
in a different color.

00:13:46.130 --> 00:13:47.480
And just looking
pretty quickly at this,

00:13:47.480 --> 00:13:49.090
I can tell this
purple service here,

00:13:49.090 --> 00:13:50.020
that's really the bottleneck.

00:13:50.020 --> 00:13:52.478
That's what's taking-- it looks
like more than five seconds

00:13:52.478 --> 00:13:56.470
of this request, so if I click
into that, that gets more data.

00:13:56.470 --> 00:13:58.380
I can see this is in--

00:13:58.380 --> 00:13:59.350
this is an API server.

00:13:59.350 --> 00:14:01.810
It's doing something
called userspace mapping,

00:14:01.810 --> 00:14:05.479
and now if I want to see
why that's happening,

00:14:05.479 --> 00:14:07.270
well I've got a bunch
of additional detail.

00:14:07.270 --> 00:14:09.160
I've got some tags, and I've got
some logs that are associated

00:14:09.160 --> 00:14:10.390
with this particular request.

00:14:10.390 --> 00:14:12.040
And I can see this
is because we're

00:14:12.040 --> 00:14:14.040
doing some sort of
connection pooling.

00:14:14.040 --> 00:14:15.370
Pool is exhausted.

00:14:15.370 --> 00:14:16.300
We stalled.

00:14:16.300 --> 00:14:17.170
YURI GRINSHTEYN: So
it's really valuable

00:14:17.170 --> 00:14:19.470
to have this information sort of
all in the same context, right?

00:14:19.470 --> 00:14:20.344
You've got the trace.

00:14:20.344 --> 00:14:23.050
For the service, it's telling
you how long things took,

00:14:23.050 --> 00:14:24.790
but then you're
pulling in the log

00:14:24.790 --> 00:14:27.720
data that's telling you exactly
why it took as long as it did.

00:14:27.720 --> 00:14:30.250
In this case, it's because--
yeah, we're out of connections.

00:14:30.250 --> 00:14:30.890
DANIEL SPOONHOWER:
Yeah, exactly.

00:14:30.890 --> 00:14:32.050
So this kind of gets
to what we think

00:14:32.050 --> 00:14:34.133
is the two kind of fundamental
activities in this.

00:14:34.133 --> 00:14:35.650
One is measurement--
understanding

00:14:35.650 --> 00:14:39.124
when something is happening, or
when something has gone wrong.

00:14:39.124 --> 00:14:40.540
And the other is
explaining what's

00:14:40.540 --> 00:14:42.360
happening and being
able to get to the root

00:14:42.360 --> 00:14:43.180
causes of those things.

00:14:43.180 --> 00:14:44.590
YURI GRINSHTEYN: So one other
thing you may need to do

00:14:44.590 --> 00:14:45.790
is to understand--
all right, so we

00:14:45.790 --> 00:14:48.460
knew that when we capture this
trace, here was the problem.

00:14:48.460 --> 00:14:50.864
How representative is
this of everything else

00:14:50.864 --> 00:14:51.530
that's going on?

00:14:51.530 --> 00:14:51.850
DANIEL SPOONHOWER:
Yeah, exactly.

00:14:51.850 --> 00:14:53.391
Like, is this still
happening, right?

00:14:53.391 --> 00:14:54.310
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Yeah.

00:14:54.310 --> 00:14:55.120
DANIEL SPOONHOWER:
One of the things--

00:14:55.120 --> 00:14:57.990
you know, we have this kind
of time travel, in a sense.

00:14:57.990 --> 00:15:00.880
What we can do is we can
look at all the requests

00:15:00.880 --> 00:15:02.680
for this particular
service that occurred

00:15:02.680 --> 00:15:05.094
over, say, the last
five or 10 minutes,

00:15:05.094 --> 00:15:06.760
and then we can kind
of break that down.

00:15:06.760 --> 00:15:09.300
Here we're showing a
histogram, not a time series,

00:15:09.300 --> 00:15:11.459
so this is trying to sort
of plot that population.

00:15:11.459 --> 00:15:14.000
And you can kind of see there's
a kind of a funny shape here.

00:15:14.000 --> 00:15:14.950
It's not totally smooth.

00:15:14.950 --> 00:15:17.449
It looks like there's sort of
maybe two distinct things that

00:15:17.449 --> 00:15:20.230
are happening, based
upon that shape,

00:15:20.230 --> 00:15:23.172
so what I can do is
I can kind of filter,

00:15:23.172 --> 00:15:24.130
look at some of those--

00:15:24.130 --> 00:15:25.900
I can compare to what
it looked like before.

00:15:25.900 --> 00:15:28.000
Maybe the shape changed a
little bit over the last hour.

00:15:28.000 --> 00:15:29.540
YURI GRINSHTEYN:
That's really useful.

00:15:29.540 --> 00:15:30.550
DANIEL SPOONHOWER:
And now I've got

00:15:30.550 --> 00:15:32.841
a bunch of examples of these
kinds of transactions that

00:15:32.841 --> 00:15:35.369
have occurred recently
in the last minute or so,

00:15:35.369 --> 00:15:37.660
and I can get a quick summary
of what the problems here

00:15:37.660 --> 00:15:38.839
might be.

00:15:38.839 --> 00:15:40.630
Again, it looks like
this userspace mapping

00:15:40.630 --> 00:15:44.067
thing is still a problem for
these slower transactions.

00:15:44.067 --> 00:15:45.650
YURI GRINSHTEYN: All
right, very cool.

00:15:45.650 --> 00:15:46.660
So this looks great.

00:15:46.660 --> 00:15:49.921
Obviously, it takes a lot of
data and a lot of processing

00:15:49.921 --> 00:15:50.920
to make all this happen.

00:15:50.920 --> 00:15:51.110
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: Yes.

00:15:51.110 --> 00:15:53.070
YURI GRINSHTEYN: So how does
LightStep actually work?

00:15:53.070 --> 00:15:54.070
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: Yeah.

00:15:54.070 --> 00:15:57.370
So we've got, kind
of logically anyway,

00:15:57.370 --> 00:16:00.250
what we think of as a
hybrid architecture.

00:16:00.250 --> 00:16:03.340
Certainly, part of LightStep
is a SaaS tool, but part of it

00:16:03.340 --> 00:16:06.050
also is an on-prem component.

00:16:06.050 --> 00:16:09.430
So we call that on-prem the
satellites, and the satellites

00:16:09.430 --> 00:16:12.750
are really gathering data from
all kinds of different sources.

00:16:12.750 --> 00:16:14.670
It could be from
your microservices.

00:16:14.670 --> 00:16:17.005
It could be that we patched
in with your monolith,

00:16:17.005 --> 00:16:18.880
because you probably
still have one of those.

00:16:18.880 --> 00:16:19.963
YURI GRINSHTEYN: A couple.

00:16:19.963 --> 00:16:22.290
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: Everyone
still has at least one.

00:16:22.290 --> 00:16:24.831
And we've even patched in with
other logging tools and things

00:16:24.831 --> 00:16:27.567
like that, as well as
gathering data from clients,

00:16:27.567 --> 00:16:29.400
and all of that data
goes to the satellites.

00:16:29.400 --> 00:16:30.730
So this is where we
were a little bit

00:16:30.730 --> 00:16:32.271
different than
something like Dapper,

00:16:32.271 --> 00:16:34.563
where even at that point
within the application itself,

00:16:34.563 --> 00:16:36.062
some sampling was
already happening,

00:16:36.062 --> 00:16:38.380
for LightStep all of this
data gets to the satellites.

00:16:38.380 --> 00:16:42.040
So the satellites are getting
this crazy fire hose of data,

00:16:42.040 --> 00:16:44.680
and they're holding onto it just
for a few minutes-- basically

00:16:44.680 --> 00:16:47.104
long enough for us, by
coordinating through the SaaS

00:16:47.104 --> 00:16:49.270
side of things, to understand
whether or not there's

00:16:49.270 --> 00:16:50.595
anything interesting there.

00:16:50.595 --> 00:16:51.730
YURI GRINSHTEYN: OK,
so tell me what's

00:16:51.730 --> 00:16:54.190
happening once the satellites
kind of do their magic-- they

00:16:54.190 --> 00:16:56.980
send the data to the cloud?

00:16:56.980 --> 00:16:58.390
What's happening there?

00:16:58.390 --> 00:16:59.890
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: So a couple
of things-- one was we're

00:16:59.890 --> 00:17:02.170
collecting these statistics,
so we can do things

00:17:02.170 --> 00:17:03.020
like show you p99.

00:17:03.020 --> 00:17:04.810
The other thing that we're
doing is sending requests

00:17:04.810 --> 00:17:06.200
back to the satellites
actually, so it's

00:17:06.200 --> 00:17:07.720
sort of a
bi-directional protocol

00:17:07.720 --> 00:17:10.510
that we do because it
might not be that something

00:17:10.510 --> 00:17:11.650
was interesting in itself.

00:17:11.650 --> 00:17:13.359
It was actually
perfectly fine, but it

00:17:13.359 --> 00:17:14.859
provides important
context for understanding

00:17:14.859 --> 00:17:16.210
why something else was slow.

00:17:16.210 --> 00:17:17.619
And we sort of-- you can
think of the satellites

00:17:17.619 --> 00:17:19.630
as kind of an online
stream processing system.

00:17:19.630 --> 00:17:21.213
YURI GRINSHTEYN: OK,
that makes sense.

00:17:21.213 --> 00:17:23.623
All right, so then the
next logical question is--

00:17:23.623 --> 00:17:26.117
the SaaS, the cloud component,
is built on Google Cloud?

00:17:26.117 --> 00:17:27.700
DANIEL SPOONHOWER:
That's right, yeah.

00:17:27.700 --> 00:17:29.242
YURI GRINSHTEYN:
How did you do that?

00:17:29.242 --> 00:17:31.366
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: I kind
of think about, you know,

00:17:31.366 --> 00:17:34.300
where we want to put our
time, as a kind of engineering

00:17:34.300 --> 00:17:35.230
organization?

00:17:35.230 --> 00:17:37.644
Where do we want to use
things off the shelf that

00:17:37.644 --> 00:17:38.560
are meeting our needs?

00:17:38.560 --> 00:17:40.976
And where do we need to add
our own kind of special sauce?

00:17:40.976 --> 00:17:42.490
YURI GRINSHTEYN:
That makes sense.

00:17:42.490 --> 00:17:44.745
So I see you've got
Kubernetes on here.

00:17:44.745 --> 00:17:48.132
You've got Cloud SQL
and Spanner as well.

00:17:48.132 --> 00:17:49.840
So I'm really interested
in all of these.

00:17:49.840 --> 00:17:51.190
Let's talk about
Kubernetes first.

00:17:51.190 --> 00:17:53.530
Obviously, this is something
that lots and lots of folks

00:17:53.530 --> 00:17:54.280
are interested in.

00:17:54.280 --> 00:17:56.310
Like, everyone is building
stuff on Kubernetes.

00:17:56.310 --> 00:17:59.050
Tell me about-- how did you
arrive at kind of building

00:17:59.050 --> 00:18:00.340
things on Kubernetes.

00:18:00.340 --> 00:18:01.510
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: If you
kind of go way, way, way

00:18:01.510 --> 00:18:03.730
back to kind of the very
beginning of LightStep,

00:18:03.730 --> 00:18:05.590
you know, we--

00:18:05.590 --> 00:18:07.300
we had our monolith
from the beginning.

00:18:07.300 --> 00:18:10.000
We didn't start with
anything too fancy.

00:18:10.000 --> 00:18:12.280
We had a little
bash shell script

00:18:12.280 --> 00:18:16.450
that we used to do
deployments, and eventually

00:18:16.450 --> 00:18:18.790
that shell script became
not such a little shell

00:18:18.790 --> 00:18:19.680
script anymore.

00:18:19.680 --> 00:18:21.430
They only put the first
few lines on here.

00:18:21.430 --> 00:18:23.764
The rest of it is a
little bit embarrassing,

00:18:23.764 --> 00:18:26.305
but we've got to a point where--
like, I was just, you know--

00:18:26.305 --> 00:18:27.490
it was getting--

00:18:27.490 --> 00:18:30.880
there's too many ways
to fail, too many ways--

00:18:30.880 --> 00:18:33.470
you know, I don't want to test
for this kind of thing, right?

00:18:33.470 --> 00:18:34.720
YURI GRINSHTEYN: That's right.

00:18:34.720 --> 00:18:36.530
Anything you're using in
production should be tested,

00:18:36.530 --> 00:18:39.140
and when you're having to write
tests for your shell scripts

00:18:39.140 --> 00:18:41.020
then you're in a tough place.

00:18:41.020 --> 00:18:41.600
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: Yeah,
not the right place.

00:18:41.600 --> 00:18:44.350
So the way I was kind of
thinking about it at that time

00:18:44.350 --> 00:18:46.570
is I wanted to kind
of leap frog ahead.

00:18:46.570 --> 00:18:48.160
Like, I didn't want to skip.

00:18:48.160 --> 00:18:50.610
You know, I don't want to date
myself by saying, you know,

00:18:50.610 --> 00:18:52.600
where I think shell
scripts come from exactly,

00:18:52.600 --> 00:18:54.944
but, you know, I didn't
want to skip to, like, 2004

00:18:54.944 --> 00:18:55.860
or anything like that.

00:18:55.860 --> 00:19:00.100
I wanted to skip right
to whatever, 2016, 2017.

00:19:00.100 --> 00:19:02.632
And so, for me,
that wasn't-- like,

00:19:02.632 --> 00:19:04.840
let's kind of set a lot of
this sort of configuration

00:19:04.840 --> 00:19:07.131
management aside and just go
straight to something like

00:19:07.131 --> 00:19:07.767
Kubernetes.

00:19:07.767 --> 00:19:10.225
YURI GRINSHTEYN: So how did
you end up deploying Kubernetes

00:19:10.225 --> 00:19:10.930
in production?

00:19:10.930 --> 00:19:12.465
Did you end up using,
like, one big cluster?

00:19:12.465 --> 00:19:14.048
Did you have lots
of smaller clusters?

00:19:14.048 --> 00:19:17.560
How did you make
those decisions?

00:19:17.560 --> 00:19:20.240
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: In several
times maybe, over and over,

00:19:20.240 --> 00:19:22.060
but I mean we started with one.

00:19:22.060 --> 00:19:24.352
I guess, obviously that's
where you always start.

00:19:24.352 --> 00:19:26.560
I think we thought about
building out a bunch of them

00:19:26.560 --> 00:19:27.790
at some point.

00:19:27.790 --> 00:19:30.410
Where I kind of arrived-- as
I think it's nice to have two,

00:19:30.410 --> 00:19:33.361
in a lot of ways.

00:19:33.361 --> 00:19:34.205
As much as I like--

00:19:34.205 --> 00:19:35.330
so we use Container Engine.

00:19:35.330 --> 00:19:36.900
The container engine
automates a lot of things,

00:19:36.900 --> 00:19:38.050
and, you, know runs the master.

00:19:38.050 --> 00:19:40.424
And like, I'm super happy that
someone else is monitoring

00:19:40.424 --> 00:19:44.740
that kind of thing for me,
but there is-- you know,

00:19:44.740 --> 00:19:46.809
you still have to
understand what's going on.

00:19:46.809 --> 00:19:48.850
Node upgrades are super
easy, but someone's still

00:19:48.850 --> 00:19:50.900
got to do it and
click that button.

00:19:50.900 --> 00:19:52.900
So I didn't really want
to be in a state, where,

00:19:52.900 --> 00:19:55.781
like, every engineer was
managing their own Kubernetes

00:19:55.781 --> 00:19:56.280
cluster.

00:19:56.280 --> 00:19:57.720
That was too many.

00:19:57.720 --> 00:19:59.470
On the other hand, I
like having a couple,

00:19:59.470 --> 00:20:02.410
so that we can test out a new
node version, things like that,

00:20:02.410 --> 00:20:03.952
in a non-customer
facing environment.

00:20:03.952 --> 00:20:05.326
YURI GRINSHTEYN:
So you basically

00:20:05.326 --> 00:20:07.650
have one call-like task and
then one production cluster

00:20:07.650 --> 00:20:08.030
kind of thing?

00:20:08.030 --> 00:20:08.460
DANIEL SPOONHOWER:
Roughly, yeah.

00:20:08.460 --> 00:20:10.380
I mean, the test one's used
for a lot of things, not

00:20:10.380 --> 00:20:12.130
just load testing and
integration testing.

00:20:12.130 --> 00:20:14.455
It's used for a bunch
of developer things.

00:20:14.455 --> 00:20:16.080
You know, if other
folks in the company

00:20:16.080 --> 00:20:18.235
want to play with stuff,
they can do it there.

00:20:18.235 --> 00:20:19.860
It let's us keep that
thing pretty open

00:20:19.860 --> 00:20:21.310
as well, because there's
no customer data in that.

00:20:21.310 --> 00:20:23.790
So it's like if you want
to play with it, go ahead.

00:20:23.790 --> 00:20:25.440
YURI GRINSHTEYN: So given
that your infrastructure is

00:20:25.440 --> 00:20:27.150
relatively straightforward,
how did you

00:20:27.150 --> 00:20:29.580
end up splitting the team
and the responsibilities

00:20:29.580 --> 00:20:30.784
to do things like--

00:20:30.784 --> 00:20:32.700
even that one cluster,
where you said it still

00:20:32.700 --> 00:20:34.366
requires management,
right-- someone has

00:20:34.366 --> 00:20:36.910
to decide to operate the nose,
versus someone else says,

00:20:36.910 --> 00:20:39.480
OK, here is the code
that we're pushing.

00:20:39.480 --> 00:20:41.689
So how did you end up, like,
divvying up those roles?

00:20:41.689 --> 00:20:43.854
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: So we
have certainly some experts

00:20:43.854 --> 00:20:45.360
in these kinds of
things, who are

00:20:45.360 --> 00:20:48.690
kind of keeping track
of that, and are

00:20:48.690 --> 00:20:51.330
aware of when we need to do
upgrades and things like that,

00:20:51.330 --> 00:20:52.830
and probably handle
a lot of that.

00:20:52.830 --> 00:20:55.380
But I didn't really want to
create a team, whose job was

00:20:55.380 --> 00:20:56.580
just to do that, in a sense.

00:20:56.580 --> 00:20:58.730
Like, maybe when we're bigger--

00:20:58.730 --> 00:21:01.260
when we get to the scale
of other companies,

00:21:01.260 --> 00:21:03.510
we'll have to do that kind
of thing, but to me-- like,

00:21:03.510 --> 00:21:05.970
I wanted to keep DevOps or SRE
to be a super collaborative

00:21:05.970 --> 00:21:08.220
kind of engagement.

00:21:08.220 --> 00:21:11.160
And that means that, like,
our SREs are actually

00:21:11.160 --> 00:21:14.265
sort of embedded in teams and
do some small amounts of product

00:21:14.265 --> 00:21:15.640
development work
as part of that,

00:21:15.640 --> 00:21:16.830
and I think that
actually helps them build

00:21:16.830 --> 00:21:18.720
a lot of empathy
for their customers

00:21:18.720 --> 00:21:20.460
with other engineers
at LightStep.

00:21:20.460 --> 00:21:21.150
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Yeah,
that makes sense.

00:21:21.150 --> 00:21:21.330
Right.

00:21:21.330 --> 00:21:23.413
When you're responsible
for supporting this thing,

00:21:23.413 --> 00:21:25.800
but you've also been the
one to figure out what

00:21:25.800 --> 00:21:26.850
push to production.

00:21:26.850 --> 00:21:29.360
You're operating on the
same SLAs and error budgets,

00:21:29.360 --> 00:21:32.190
and shared empathy
is super important.

00:21:32.190 --> 00:21:33.320
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: Exactly.

00:21:33.320 --> 00:21:34.611
YURI GRINSHTEYN: OK, very cool.

00:21:34.611 --> 00:21:36.130
Now let's talk
about the back end.

00:21:36.130 --> 00:21:38.130
You've got a lot of data
coming into the system.

00:21:38.130 --> 00:21:38.580
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: That's right.

00:21:38.580 --> 00:21:39.240
YURI GRINSHTEYN:
Where is it going?

00:21:39.240 --> 00:21:40.440
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: Yeah,
that's the hard part, I think.

00:21:40.440 --> 00:21:42.380
If you don't have
persistence, like, everything

00:21:42.380 --> 00:21:43.296
is pretty easy, right?

00:21:43.296 --> 00:21:44.430
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Right.

00:21:44.430 --> 00:21:46.888
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: So I mean,
there's some stories in this,

00:21:46.888 --> 00:21:47.790
I think.

00:21:47.790 --> 00:21:52.680
Back in the day when LightStep
was just a demo and just

00:21:52.680 --> 00:21:56.450
a pilot, we started just
developing from our laptops.

00:21:56.450 --> 00:21:58.900
And so, we were
running MySQL locally.

00:21:58.900 --> 00:22:00.490
It's pretty easy.

00:22:00.490 --> 00:22:02.617
And we put a ton
of data in there--

00:22:02.617 --> 00:22:04.700
probably some data that
shouldn't have been there,

00:22:04.700 --> 00:22:05.741
but it was a demo, right?

00:22:05.741 --> 00:22:07.050
So you're just moving fast.

00:22:07.050 --> 00:22:09.319
I think this is the thing
that always kind of like--

00:22:09.319 --> 00:22:10.860
not haunts me but
I think back about.

00:22:10.860 --> 00:22:12.401
Like, we've been
kind of continuously

00:22:12.401 --> 00:22:14.870
operating LightStep from those
very early pilot days, and--

00:22:14.870 --> 00:22:17.370
YURI GRINSHTEYN: I mean, that's
really fairly common, right?

00:22:17.370 --> 00:22:20.970
In startups, you often come out
of something like a hackathon.

00:22:20.970 --> 00:22:23.610
You've got this thing
that you built overnight,

00:22:23.610 --> 00:22:26.460
and suddenly you're doing, you
know, sales calls to customers

00:22:26.460 --> 00:22:28.080
and going in production.

00:22:28.080 --> 00:22:28.350
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: Exactly.

00:22:28.350 --> 00:22:30.308
Part of me-- you know,
the engineer deep inside

00:22:30.308 --> 00:22:32.696
wants to, like, make a clean
start, and start on B2,

00:22:32.696 --> 00:22:33.570
and things like that.

00:22:33.570 --> 00:22:35.080
But I just-- you
know, there's no time,

00:22:35.080 --> 00:22:36.430
and it's probably not
the right thing anyway.

00:22:36.430 --> 00:22:37.410
YURI GRINSHTEYN: It's
never the right time

00:22:37.410 --> 00:22:39.020
to start from scratch.

00:22:39.020 --> 00:22:41.310
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: So a lot
of the stuff is in MySQL--

00:22:41.310 --> 00:22:42.420
definitely some things
that shouldn't be.

00:22:42.420 --> 00:22:44.670
One of those was a bunch
of time series data--

00:22:44.670 --> 00:22:47.190
not super high frequency but,
like, still time series data

00:22:47.190 --> 00:22:48.580
itself.

00:22:48.580 --> 00:22:50.910
And when we moved into
Cloud, we were really

00:22:50.910 --> 00:22:55.500
starting to overwhelm the
Cloud SQL servers with just

00:22:55.500 --> 00:22:57.865
the write rate, and keeping
up with replication,

00:22:57.865 --> 00:22:58.740
and things like that.

00:22:58.740 --> 00:22:59.010
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Yeah.

00:22:59.010 --> 00:23:00.690
So it's interesting that
you bring up time series

00:23:00.690 --> 00:23:02.010
and write rate,
because whenever I

00:23:02.010 --> 00:23:04.135
talk to customers that have
that particular problem

00:23:04.135 --> 00:23:06.270
in their environment, I
would say, hey, Bigtable

00:23:06.270 --> 00:23:08.460
was built to do just that.

00:23:08.460 --> 00:23:10.200
And you've got it
on your slide here,

00:23:10.200 --> 00:23:12.230
so you were kind of
doing my pitch for me.

00:23:12.230 --> 00:23:13.230
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: Yeah.

00:23:13.230 --> 00:23:14.970
We actually took
kind of a long path

00:23:14.970 --> 00:23:17.655
through these, which
maybe not totally evident.

00:23:17.655 --> 00:23:19.530
And so, we actually
moved to Cassandra first.

00:23:19.530 --> 00:23:21.590
This was actually before
Bigtable was a product,

00:23:21.590 --> 00:23:24.720
so that was sort of
where we were first

00:23:24.720 --> 00:23:25.990
feeling the pain around this.

00:23:25.990 --> 00:23:28.590
And Cassandra was great in a
lot of ways, though I think--

00:23:28.590 --> 00:23:31.500
in retrospect, I never want
to sort of underestimate

00:23:31.500 --> 00:23:34.380
the amount of expertise that's
required to run a distributed

00:23:34.380 --> 00:23:35.475
storage system.

00:23:35.475 --> 00:23:36.640
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Yeah,
that makes sense.

00:23:36.640 --> 00:23:38.670
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: So we
actually got into a pretty bad

00:23:38.670 --> 00:23:39.480
state with Cassandra.

00:23:39.480 --> 00:23:41.410
So we run a couple of
different environments.

00:23:41.410 --> 00:23:43.050
One of them we
call Meta, which is

00:23:43.050 --> 00:23:46.019
the LightStep environment that
monitors the other LightStep

00:23:46.019 --> 00:23:46.560
environments.

00:23:46.560 --> 00:23:47.490
YURI GRINSHTEYN: OK,
that sounds important.

00:23:47.490 --> 00:23:47.760
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: Yeah.

00:23:47.760 --> 00:23:49.820
Yeah, I mean, for us it's
important to understand.

00:23:49.820 --> 00:23:51.810
Our customers depend on
us to be up and understand

00:23:51.810 --> 00:23:52.393
their systems.

00:23:52.393 --> 00:23:54.270
We've got to be available.

00:23:54.270 --> 00:23:55.860
So the Cassandra
cluster that we were

00:23:55.860 --> 00:23:58.050
running as part of the Meta
environment got into a state

00:23:58.050 --> 00:23:59.591
where one of the
nodes was corrupted,

00:23:59.591 --> 00:24:02.340
and we had not done a great job
with some of the configuration.

00:24:02.340 --> 00:24:05.880
And it was basically going to
be a significant outage for us

00:24:05.880 --> 00:24:07.830
to recover that thing,
and that, again,

00:24:07.830 --> 00:24:10.140
a non-customer
facing environment.

00:24:10.140 --> 00:24:11.990
So we could make it work.

00:24:11.990 --> 00:24:14.490
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Probably not
a pattern you'd want to be in.

00:24:14.490 --> 00:24:15.210
DANIEL SPOONHOWER:
Yeah, exactly.

00:24:15.210 --> 00:24:16.830
I didn't-- you know, what
was going to stop this from

00:24:16.830 --> 00:24:19.830
happening [INAUDIBLE],, so we
actually wanted to move pretty

00:24:19.830 --> 00:24:22.740
quickly to get out of that state
and migrate our data elsewhere.

00:24:22.740 --> 00:24:26.760
We actually did some
evaluation of Bigtable

00:24:26.760 --> 00:24:29.670
and of Spanner at that
point and kind of looked at

00:24:29.670 --> 00:24:32.340
like, you know, what are
the costs going to be?

00:24:32.340 --> 00:24:35.740
What are the, sort of, overhead
in terms of the latency

00:24:35.740 --> 00:24:37.740
that that's going to
introduce into our product,

00:24:37.740 --> 00:24:39.330
and what are the kind
of different features

00:24:39.330 --> 00:24:40.600
that are available
on those things?

00:24:40.600 --> 00:24:41.610
YURI GRINSHTEYN: So I actually
want to come back to Spanner,

00:24:41.610 --> 00:24:43.509
but tell me more
about that evaluation.

00:24:43.509 --> 00:24:45.300
So how did you do it,
and what did you see?

00:24:45.300 --> 00:24:46.100
DANIEL SPOONHOWER:
We actually used

00:24:46.100 --> 00:24:48.847
LightStep itself to do some
of that evaluation, which

00:24:48.847 --> 00:24:49.430
is pretty fun.

00:24:49.430 --> 00:24:52.950
So this is just showing latency
for the reads against Cassandra

00:24:52.950 --> 00:24:54.380
and against Spanner.

00:24:54.380 --> 00:24:57.240
The Bigtable ones-- we ended
up reproducing some of this

00:24:57.240 --> 00:24:59.490
actually-- looked fairly
similar to the Cassandra one.

00:24:59.490 --> 00:25:02.292
[? And ?] a lot of this is
maybe not super surprisingly--

00:25:02.292 --> 00:25:03.750
but yeah, I think
this kind of gets

00:25:03.750 --> 00:25:05.791
back to what we were
talking about before-- about

00:25:05.791 --> 00:25:09.600
the difference between
p50 and p99 latency.

00:25:09.600 --> 00:25:11.750
If you look at the
median latency,

00:25:11.750 --> 00:25:13.150
Cassandra is a
little bit faster,

00:25:13.150 --> 00:25:14.100
which is sort of expected.

00:25:14.100 --> 00:25:15.141
It's in the same cluster.

00:25:15.141 --> 00:25:16.960
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Yeah,
it's not surprising.

00:25:16.960 --> 00:25:18.210
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: And
so, it was looking--

00:25:18.210 --> 00:25:20.190
median was about 2 and
1/2 to 2 milliseconds.

00:25:20.190 --> 00:25:20.790
YURI GRINSHTEYN:
That's pretty fast.

00:25:20.790 --> 00:25:22.510
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: Yeah,
it was great and cool.

00:25:22.510 --> 00:25:24.570
Spanner's a little bit slower--
about 6 or 7 milliseconds.

00:25:24.570 --> 00:25:25.200
YURI GRINSHTEYN:
Still pretty fast.

00:25:25.200 --> 00:25:26.700
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: Still
pretty fast, totally within

00:25:26.700 --> 00:25:29.080
bounds for what we needed
in terms of our application

00:25:29.080 --> 00:25:31.050
in the performance
[? feed. ?] But if you

00:25:31.050 --> 00:25:32.670
look at those
longer-tailed latencies,

00:25:32.670 --> 00:25:34.500
actually there is
this huge spread

00:25:34.500 --> 00:25:37.214
kind of shown in the
graph here by the distance

00:25:37.214 --> 00:25:38.880
between the lines on
the Cassandra side.

00:25:38.880 --> 00:25:41.312
That p99 latency was two
orders of magnitude bigger.

00:25:41.312 --> 00:25:43.395
YURI GRINSHTEYN: That's a
pretty big distribution.

00:25:43.395 --> 00:25:44.978
DANIEL SPOONHOWER:
Yeah, so 1% of them

00:25:44.978 --> 00:25:46.999
were actually 250 milliseconds.

00:25:46.999 --> 00:25:48.790
On the Spanner side,
it's incredibly tight.

00:25:48.790 --> 00:25:51.240
So Spanner had this really
predictable behavior,

00:25:51.240 --> 00:25:53.409
and to us that was kind
of a comforting thing.

00:25:53.409 --> 00:25:55.950
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Yeah, I think--
the decision you made right.

00:25:55.950 --> 00:25:58.530
It's better to be slightly
slower but completely

00:25:58.530 --> 00:26:02.224
predictably so than a bit faster
but much less predictable.

00:26:02.224 --> 00:26:03.640
DANIEL SPOONHOWER:
Yeah, and there

00:26:03.640 --> 00:26:06.999
were some other features around
kind of that replication that

00:26:06.999 --> 00:26:09.540
were available at Spanner, at
the time anyway, that were also

00:26:09.540 --> 00:26:10.210
pretty attractive to us.

00:26:10.210 --> 00:26:11.430
YURI GRINSHTEYN: All right,
so let's go back a step,

00:26:11.430 --> 00:26:13.530
because one of the things
I'm really interested in

00:26:13.530 --> 00:26:15.857
is kind of how you were
using Spanner, right?

00:26:15.857 --> 00:26:17.940
This is a conversation
that I have with customers,

00:26:17.940 --> 00:26:19.314
pretty frequently,
that basically

00:26:19.314 --> 00:26:21.120
think that Spanner
is basically kind

00:26:21.120 --> 00:26:22.900
of the next relational database.

00:26:22.900 --> 00:26:25.800
And so if I'm in something like
Cloud SQL today, then Spanner--

00:26:25.800 --> 00:26:28.369
you know, once I get to a
certain size, Spanner's just

00:26:28.369 --> 00:26:29.910
sort of the logical
next step, right?

00:26:29.910 --> 00:26:30.040
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: Yeah.

00:26:30.040 --> 00:26:32.498
YURI GRINSHTEYN: And they just
think of it as-- it's a big,

00:26:32.498 --> 00:26:36.334
sort of globally consistent
relational database,

00:26:36.334 --> 00:26:38.750
but the thing they really care
about is that it's just a--

00:26:38.750 --> 00:26:40.130
it's like a fully manageable
relational database.

00:26:40.130 --> 00:26:40.480
DANIEL SPOONHOWER:
Exactly, yeah.

00:26:40.480 --> 00:26:41.940
YURI GRINSHTEYN: And then
you have different customers

00:26:41.940 --> 00:26:44.970
or different use cases, where
what they really care about

00:26:44.970 --> 00:26:47.436
is that global consistency.

00:26:47.436 --> 00:26:48.810
That's absolutely
what they need,

00:26:48.810 --> 00:26:51.720
and the only way you get
that, [? certainly, ?] in GCP

00:26:51.720 --> 00:26:52.770
is on Spanner.

00:26:52.770 --> 00:26:54.180
And that [? would ?] be the only
[? thing ?] given in the world,

00:26:54.180 --> 00:26:55.055
[? quite honestly. ?]

00:26:55.055 --> 00:26:56.130
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: Yes.

00:26:56.130 --> 00:26:58.046
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Where
did you kind of end up?

00:26:58.046 --> 00:27:00.060
How did you make your
decision to go to Spanner?

00:27:00.060 --> 00:27:02.476
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: It was kind
of a mix of those, I think.

00:27:02.476 --> 00:27:04.610
So part of it was we were
being a bit reactive.

00:27:04.610 --> 00:27:07.430
We know we had this cluster
that could fail at any moment.

00:27:07.430 --> 00:27:08.260
YURI GRINSHTEYN: You
needed to do something.

00:27:08.260 --> 00:27:09.420
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: Yes, so
we needed to do something,

00:27:09.420 --> 00:27:11.880
and I think Spanner
had the flexibility

00:27:11.880 --> 00:27:14.980
to make that
migration pretty easy.

00:27:14.980 --> 00:27:18.042
I think for all these
things, you know,

00:27:18.042 --> 00:27:19.500
LightStep is going
to change a lot.

00:27:19.500 --> 00:27:22.090
We're going to build some
new cool stuff in the future.

00:27:22.090 --> 00:27:24.270
Google Cloud is going to offer a
lot of cool stuff in the future

00:27:24.270 --> 00:27:24.769
too.

00:27:24.769 --> 00:27:27.000
We're going to make more
migrations, and so to me

00:27:27.000 --> 00:27:29.166
the thing that was really
attractive about Spanner--

00:27:29.166 --> 00:27:31.640
I guess, part of it was the
kind of global replication.

00:27:31.640 --> 00:27:34.260
And that made us feel good
about not having the data

00:27:34.260 --> 00:27:35.972
wherever we needed it--

00:27:35.972 --> 00:27:37.680
but really putting us
in control in terms

00:27:37.680 --> 00:27:39.810
of when we were going
to have to migrate again

00:27:39.810 --> 00:27:42.430
and knowing that it was going
to scale to whatever we needed.

00:27:42.430 --> 00:27:43.470
YURI GRINSHTEYN:
Yeah, it's much better

00:27:43.470 --> 00:27:45.531
to do the migration when
you think you're ready

00:27:45.531 --> 00:27:47.280
rather than when your
infrastructure says.

00:27:47.280 --> 00:27:48.000
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: Exactly.

00:27:48.000 --> 00:27:49.320
So we can plan-- you
know, this quarter, we're

00:27:49.320 --> 00:27:50.610
going to do feature, or
we're going to do migration.

00:27:50.610 --> 00:27:52.568
No, we're going to push
the migration off to p1

00:27:52.568 --> 00:27:54.872
and be comfortable
knowing that solution's

00:27:54.872 --> 00:27:55.830
going to scale to that.

00:27:55.830 --> 00:27:56.970
YURI GRINSHTEYN: All
right, very cool.

00:27:56.970 --> 00:27:58.130
All right, so we've
talked about-- you know,

00:27:58.130 --> 00:28:00.120
actually, I think I want to ask
one more question that I forgot

00:28:00.120 --> 00:28:02.017
to ask about on the
Kubernetes, and I

00:28:02.017 --> 00:28:03.850
don't know if you want
to get into this now.

00:28:03.850 --> 00:28:06.266
Can you tell me a little about
how you doing multi-tenancy

00:28:06.266 --> 00:28:07.240
in Kubernetes?

00:28:07.240 --> 00:28:09.220
DANIEL SPOONHOWER:
Yeah, it's a mix.

00:28:09.220 --> 00:28:12.420
So we have some things,
where we do shared tenancy,

00:28:12.420 --> 00:28:15.757
where there's relatively
low overhead in terms of--

00:28:15.757 --> 00:28:17.340
well, maybe not low
overhead but just,

00:28:17.340 --> 00:28:20.280
like, low computational costs
of what we needed to do.

00:28:20.280 --> 00:28:22.440
And we also have cases,
where we want to more--

00:28:22.440 --> 00:28:24.300
isolation-- and we
use a combination

00:28:24.300 --> 00:28:27.009
of labels and some tainting
to make that happen.

00:28:27.009 --> 00:28:28.050
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Awesome.

00:28:28.050 --> 00:28:32.040
All right, so we're starting to
get kind of near the end here.

00:28:32.040 --> 00:28:33.810
This is a lot of
really good stuff here.

00:28:33.810 --> 00:28:36.270
I think you've learned some
really interesting, really

00:28:36.270 --> 00:28:39.270
important lessons as you kind
of first got into the Cloud

00:28:39.270 --> 00:28:41.160
and then kind of made
some technology changes

00:28:41.160 --> 00:28:42.630
and technology migrations.

00:28:42.630 --> 00:28:44.946
You know, if you were to
leave our audience with just

00:28:44.946 --> 00:28:46.320
a couple of things
that you think

00:28:46.320 --> 00:28:48.930
they should learn from your
experience, what would it be?

00:28:48.930 --> 00:28:50.130
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: Yes,
so I think one of them

00:28:50.130 --> 00:28:51.296
you were asking about a bit.

00:28:51.296 --> 00:28:53.490
Like, what was the kind
of cluster architecture

00:28:53.490 --> 00:28:54.919
that we set up?

00:28:54.919 --> 00:28:56.460
I really like, for
all of these kinds

00:28:56.460 --> 00:28:58.800
of questions, the number two,
because one's probably not

00:28:58.800 --> 00:28:59.300
enough.

00:28:59.300 --> 00:29:02.010
It doesn't give you a
chance to test things out.

00:29:02.010 --> 00:29:03.234
10 or 20 is probably a lot.

00:29:03.234 --> 00:29:04.650
YURI GRINSHTEYN:
It's getting back

00:29:04.650 --> 00:29:05.430
to what you're
talking about, where

00:29:05.430 --> 00:29:06.990
you don't want every
engineer to have

00:29:06.990 --> 00:29:08.010
to manage their own cluster.

00:29:08.010 --> 00:29:08.880
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: Exactly.

00:29:08.880 --> 00:29:10.710
But I think going back,
there's some things

00:29:10.710 --> 00:29:13.130
that I might have thought
a little bit harder about,

00:29:13.130 --> 00:29:14.640
and I would encourage
other people

00:29:14.640 --> 00:29:16.427
to do-- in thinking
about how are you

00:29:16.427 --> 00:29:18.510
going to be able to attribute
these infrastructure

00:29:18.510 --> 00:29:19.616
costs to your customers?

00:29:19.616 --> 00:29:21.490
Is that something that
is interesting to you?

00:29:21.490 --> 00:29:23.489
I bet that's going to be
interesting to your CFO

00:29:23.489 --> 00:29:24.570
someday--

00:29:24.570 --> 00:29:26.820
thinking about how you're
breaking up billing in terms

00:29:26.820 --> 00:29:30.217
of where are you spending
money on customer facing

00:29:30.217 --> 00:29:32.800
environments, and where are you
spending money on a load test?

00:29:32.800 --> 00:29:34.799
And just being able to
understand that over time

00:29:34.799 --> 00:29:36.150
is really important.

00:29:36.150 --> 00:29:39.000
I think that's influenced
some of our decisions

00:29:39.000 --> 00:29:41.125
around Kubernetes, but also
in the storage itself--

00:29:41.125 --> 00:29:42.541
when do you create
a new instance?

00:29:42.541 --> 00:29:43.900
When do you make a new bucket?

00:29:43.900 --> 00:29:46.000
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Yeah,
that makes sense.

00:29:46.000 --> 00:29:46.230
DANIEL SPOONHOWER:
The other thing

00:29:46.230 --> 00:29:47.938
I was thinking about
a bit, and something

00:29:47.938 --> 00:29:50.340
I would like to continue to
do better for us even is just

00:29:50.340 --> 00:29:52.290
kind of step back and think
about that big picture

00:29:52.290 --> 00:29:52.915
around storage.

00:29:52.915 --> 00:29:54.540
Like, I've sort of
described in terms

00:29:54.540 --> 00:29:59.010
of how we got to Spanner--
like, that code followed

00:29:59.010 --> 00:30:03.170
this path from MySQL to
Cassandra and eventually

00:30:03.170 --> 00:30:03.679
to Spanner.

00:30:03.679 --> 00:30:05.220
And you know, there's
a legacy there,

00:30:05.220 --> 00:30:07.500
in a sense that we've-- that
code was originally written

00:30:07.500 --> 00:30:08.850
with a very different
set of constraints.

00:30:08.850 --> 00:30:10.070
YURI GRINSHTEYN: That's right.

00:30:10.070 --> 00:30:11.820
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: And
I think if you were

00:30:11.820 --> 00:30:13.470
going to be at a point where--

00:30:13.470 --> 00:30:16.830
you're going to be on one
of these hosted storage

00:30:16.830 --> 00:30:18.844
systems-- which I
think is a great thing

00:30:18.844 --> 00:30:20.760
about productivity--
really thinking about how

00:30:20.760 --> 00:30:23.610
the features that are only
available in those platforms

00:30:23.610 --> 00:30:24.960
could actually influence
the kinds of features

00:30:24.960 --> 00:30:26.990
that you've built. So some of
the more interesting features

00:30:26.990 --> 00:30:28.410
of Spanner, things
around BigQuery--

00:30:28.410 --> 00:30:29.340
like, there's a lot
of things, that you

00:30:29.340 --> 00:30:30.881
can do that are
really cool, that you

00:30:30.881 --> 00:30:32.420
can't do on your laptop.

00:30:32.420 --> 00:30:34.170
And that, I think,
would be great to think

00:30:34.170 --> 00:30:36.240
about when you're designing
features in that product

00:30:36.240 --> 00:30:36.840
you want to build.

00:30:36.840 --> 00:30:38.550
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Yeah, and
obviously the trade off there,

00:30:38.550 --> 00:30:40.580
then, is this concept
of lock in, right?

00:30:40.580 --> 00:30:44.990
If you were building stuff
with specific GCP features,

00:30:44.990 --> 00:30:46.620
you get a lot of
value out of that,

00:30:46.620 --> 00:30:48.880
but then you're sort of
tied to the platform.

00:30:48.880 --> 00:30:50.380
DANIEL SPOONHOWER:
Yeah, and I think

00:30:50.380 --> 00:30:51.867
that's a trade off to make.

00:30:51.867 --> 00:30:53.700
The way I think of it
is just, like, there's

00:30:53.700 --> 00:30:55.241
a lot of different
features out there

00:30:55.241 --> 00:30:57.782
and take advantage
of the best thing.

00:30:57.782 --> 00:30:59.670
As much as like Cassandra
doesn't lock you

00:30:59.670 --> 00:31:01.890
in in any way-- like,
there's a set of expertise

00:31:01.890 --> 00:31:03.230
that we would have had
to build around that,

00:31:03.230 --> 00:31:04.560
and that's an
investment there that we

00:31:04.560 --> 00:31:05.710
would have to make if they
really wanted to do that.

00:31:05.710 --> 00:31:07.030
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Yeah, and
then the bigger you get,

00:31:07.030 --> 00:31:08.405
the more critical
about Cassandra

00:31:08.405 --> 00:31:10.041
system is to your customers--

00:31:10.041 --> 00:31:11.790
the more you have to
invest in making sure

00:31:11.790 --> 00:31:15.080
that you have folks on staff
that can build it, manage it,

00:31:15.080 --> 00:31:15.610
run it.

00:31:15.610 --> 00:31:15.740
DANIEL SPOONHOWER:
So you're going

00:31:15.740 --> 00:31:17.460
to make that investment
one way or the other.

00:31:17.460 --> 00:31:18.060
YURI GRINSHTEYN: OK, cool.

00:31:18.060 --> 00:31:18.690
All right.

00:31:18.690 --> 00:31:19.700
Well, thank you so much.

00:31:19.700 --> 00:31:20.490
We really appreciate it.

00:31:20.490 --> 00:31:21.280
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: Yeah.

00:31:21.280 --> 00:31:23.075
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Please
stay tuned for live Q&amp;A,

00:31:23.075 --> 00:31:24.741
and we'll be back in
less than a minute.

00:32:41.700 --> 00:32:42.790
All right, welcome back.

00:32:42.790 --> 00:32:44.520
Let's go through some
of our questions.

00:32:44.520 --> 00:32:47.170
So first question we have is
around machine learning, right.

00:32:47.170 --> 00:32:50.140
So are you considering to
using, kind of, machine learning

00:32:50.140 --> 00:32:52.480
to improve some
of the information

00:32:52.480 --> 00:32:54.070
that you're providing
to your users?

00:32:54.070 --> 00:32:54.330
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: Yeah.

00:32:54.330 --> 00:32:56.060
This is a question we
get a lot actually.

00:32:56.060 --> 00:32:58.060
Machine learning and
observability I think

00:32:58.060 --> 00:32:59.360
is a really tricky thing.

00:32:59.360 --> 00:33:00.276
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Yeah.

00:33:01.789 --> 00:33:04.330
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: Being wrong
in a way where you've woken up

00:33:04.330 --> 00:33:05.860
an engineer at 3
o'clock in the morning--

00:33:05.860 --> 00:33:06.860
oh, no, it wasn't a problem.

00:33:06.860 --> 00:33:06.990
Sorry.

00:33:06.990 --> 00:33:07.810
YURI GRINSHTEYN: That's right.

00:33:07.810 --> 00:33:08.850
DANIEL SPOONHOWER:
It's pretty different,

00:33:08.850 --> 00:33:10.750
and the confidence
that an engineer is

00:33:10.750 --> 00:33:12.291
going to have in
that system is going

00:33:12.291 --> 00:33:15.160
to go down really fast for
those kinds of false positives.

00:33:15.160 --> 00:33:16.330
I think the way that we
think about LightStep

00:33:16.330 --> 00:33:17.496
a lot is kind of as a guide.

00:33:17.496 --> 00:33:19.840
So in other places where
we can use machine learning

00:33:19.840 --> 00:33:24.154
to better surface the kinds
of facts and sort of evidence

00:33:24.154 --> 00:33:25.570
that can help you
make a decision,

00:33:25.570 --> 00:33:27.190
but really putting those
facts in front of you

00:33:27.190 --> 00:33:28.480
for you, as a human to choose.

00:33:28.480 --> 00:33:29.980
Because in the
end, like, you just

00:33:29.980 --> 00:33:31.570
have state that
the machines don't

00:33:31.570 --> 00:33:34.870
have, in terms of the where
that release has happened,

00:33:34.870 --> 00:33:37.770
and sort of the organization,
how the software's developed,

00:33:37.770 --> 00:33:39.050
all those kinds of things.

00:33:39.050 --> 00:33:39.640
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Yeah,
and if you kind of

00:33:39.640 --> 00:33:41.090
go back to things like
that-- [? sort of ?]

00:33:41.090 --> 00:33:41.930
your principles, right?

00:33:41.930 --> 00:33:44.263
The idea is that you want to
spend all the time upfront,

00:33:44.263 --> 00:33:46.121
identifying your
SLOs, your SLIs.

00:33:46.121 --> 00:33:48.120
And then when you're doing
things like alerting,

00:33:48.120 --> 00:33:49.630
it's obviously
something that you've

00:33:49.630 --> 00:33:51.130
designed very intentionally.

00:33:51.130 --> 00:33:52.255
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: Exactly.

00:33:52.255 --> 00:33:53.050
Exactly, yeah.

00:33:53.050 --> 00:33:54.091
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Awesome.

00:33:54.091 --> 00:33:56.824
All right, so we started out
talking about microservices.

00:33:56.824 --> 00:33:58.990
Did you build LightStep on
from using microservices?

00:33:58.990 --> 00:34:00.989
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: Yeah,
not from the beginning.

00:34:00.989 --> 00:34:05.500
I think we started from a single
statically-linked go binary,

00:34:05.500 --> 00:34:07.660
and that actually-- you
know, we actually still

00:34:07.660 --> 00:34:10.199
have pieces of that
today, which, you know,

00:34:10.199 --> 00:34:13.631
it's living on, and it's
sort of a continual challenge

00:34:13.631 --> 00:34:15.130
in the place where
we have to invest

00:34:15.130 --> 00:34:16.409
in our own infrastructure.

00:34:16.409 --> 00:34:18.519
But yeah, I don't think--

00:34:18.519 --> 00:34:21.400
I mean, as much as microservices
can be great in a lot of ways

00:34:21.400 --> 00:34:23.627
if you were one person
sitting at your laptop,

00:34:23.627 --> 00:34:24.960
microservices are about scaling.

00:34:24.960 --> 00:34:27.580
They're about dividing
up your application

00:34:27.580 --> 00:34:30.340
in a way that, like, maps into
your organizational structure,

00:34:30.340 --> 00:34:30.840
right?

00:34:30.840 --> 00:34:31.870
They're about fault isolation.

00:34:31.870 --> 00:34:33.820
If you're running everything on
your laptop with you coding it,

00:34:33.820 --> 00:34:35.429
like none of those things
are valuable, right?

00:34:35.429 --> 00:34:35.699
YURI GRINSHTEYN: That's right.

00:34:35.699 --> 00:34:38.050
And so, it's something you
have to have a lot of foresight

00:34:38.050 --> 00:34:40.060
when you're first starting out,
so if you're literally just one

00:34:40.060 --> 00:34:41.590
person building
on your laptop you

00:34:41.590 --> 00:34:43.048
have to think about
what is my life

00:34:43.048 --> 00:34:45.760
going-- if this thing
works, what is my life going

00:34:45.760 --> 00:34:47.409
to be like a year from now?

00:34:47.409 --> 00:34:47.630
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: Yeah.

00:34:47.630 --> 00:34:48.550
YURI GRINSHTEYN: And
you have to think--

00:34:48.550 --> 00:34:50.090
if you want to kind
of go down that route,

00:34:50.090 --> 00:34:52.000
you have to be very
much forward thinking,

00:34:52.000 --> 00:34:54.159
and it's hard to do that when
you're a person on your own.

00:34:54.159 --> 00:34:54.340
DANIEL SPOONHOWER:
Yeah, and there's

00:34:54.340 --> 00:34:56.830
an investment in doing that,
because that slows things down.

00:34:56.830 --> 00:34:57.560
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Yeah,
all of that is overhead.

00:34:57.560 --> 00:34:59.740
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: You've got
to deploy multiple services

00:34:59.740 --> 00:35:01.739
before it was just a push
